A mutable paragraph style must own a MUTABLE tab stop array, and setTabStops must not mutate.

NSMutableParagraphStyle mutates _tabStops IN PLACE: setTabStops did removeAllObjects then
addObjectsFromArray, and addTabStop and removeTabStop still insert and remove. _initWithDefaults
takes a -mutableCopy and gets that right. The other two initialisers did not:

  -initWithParagraphStyle:  _tabStops = [other->_tabStops copy]      an IMMUTABLE NSArray
  -initWithCoder:           _tabStops = [[... @"Tabs"] retain]       whatever the archive held

So a mutable style built by copying another one, which is what -mutableCopy does and what any
application does before adjusting indents, raised NSInternalInconsistencyException "attempting to
mutate an immutable object" from -[__NSCFArray removeAllObjects] on the first setTabStops.

MEASURED, with CIDER_TRACE_EXCEPTIONS on an iA Writer drive that opens a document:

  -[__NSCFArray removeAllObjects]
  -[NSMutableParagraphStyle setTabStops:]
  -[IATextController adjustIndentOnParagraphStyle:shouldHang:indentWrapped:forBlockElementWithMarkerRange:]
  -[IATextController handleHeadingInRange:markerRange:]
  -[IAMarkdownLiveParser parseLoopInRange:]
  -[IAMarkdownLiveParser stringDidChangeInRange:delta:]

iA Writer runs that live parser on EVERY string change and CATCHES the exception, so the markdown
parse of a heading simply stopped, silently, one frame at a time.

Both initialisers now take a mutableCopy, and setTabStops REPLACES the array rather than mutating
it, so it no longer depends on what the last initialiser happened to leave behind.

AFTER: that raise goes from 1 to 0 in the same drive. BE CLEAR ABOUT WHAT THAT DID NOT FIX: the
editor pane is still empty, because the document text and the editor view are on two different text
storages, which is a separate defect (#194).
